# Prospective Notes trash publication correction

This work-only candidate references the immutable325-file Notes trash stage and the independent37-file review using exact manifest identities in `baseline-reference.json`. It copies only57 selected runnable/shared fixture and original study files, not recursive historical results. Production, shared host, requirements, prior receipts and both frozen evidence folders remain unchanged.

The client captures an editor generation, all four draft values and an input revision for Save. An older completion cannot replace a subsequently accepted/trashed/new editor. New enabled-field input, including edit-then-undo, retains its draft; the already committed Save revision is retained internally so a subsequent save uses the correct optimistic revision. A Save completion after the refresh cannot replace newer dirty status. Both asynchronous refresh reads use an independent request generation, so an old active list or notebook state cannot republish after a newer cascade/search refresh. This is publication guarding, not cancellation or rollback of an already committed request.

The server captures the new notebook Trash/Restore action, ID, revision and confirmation as four immutable primitives before the serialization await. Caller alias mutation cannot retarget those two commands. No broad input ownership guarantee is claimed for pre-existing Notes actions.

Preserved initial adapter run:7 tests,5pass/2fail reproducing the independent P1 draft loss/stale Save enablement and P2 caller alias retargeting. The first correction passed7/7. A separate held-out run then found an adjacent stale-list case,5pass/1fail: the backend note remained trashed and Save remained disabled, but an old post-Save list republished the note button after the newer cascade refresh. Its exact source and raw trace are retained before the added list guard; the next run passed6/6. The final corpus adds a second-refresh-await control. The same exact reviewer seven bodies remain unchanged except stage import paths.

Native trash/restore algorithms, JEX serialization, Joplin source/helper bytes, shared host and state-store bytes are unchanged from the accepted original325 source study. Released Joplin3.6.14 commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d` comparison scope stays exact source slices with bounded owned SQL/load/save adapters and a fixed clock. No released desktop/database/sync/GUI executable is run. The source-assisted runtime remains AGPL-3.0-or-later; licensing/provenance are retained by exact selected source bytes and baseline references. This correction adds no new original contract or full notebook parity claim.

Existing bounds and gaps remain:1,000 notes,64 hierarchy levels,200-character notebook path bound,8MiB JEX,4MiB individual resources,20 local history rows, atomic single-state persistence after a validated bounded cascade. Shared/encrypted/conflict/special notebooks, permanent deletion/resource garbage collection, sync transactions, multi-client coordination and complete lifecycle cancellation remain outside this subset. Enabled fields preserve newer text under the tested Save/cascade boundaries; this is not a universal editor lifecycle guarantee. No timing, memory, security or superiority claim is made.
